#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "c02ce0b7-d660-5d31-8147-303a9e6f806d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1223" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$?\n\n1. The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $y\\gt 0$.\n2. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $\\left(1,0.2\\right)$.\n3. $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$." :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$ is defined for all input values $x$, and, the set of possible outputs consists of all the positive numbers.\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=1$, we have\n$f(1) = (0.2)^1 = 0.2$.\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ decreases, the function value gets larger and larger, approaching *positive* infinity. Therefore $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/id #uuid "90054a4d-adcf-56b8-ab73-86048bc0f4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1224" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$." :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](images/f7/f75c2ffd657117da715cd6ec476e39b073dc24e069f1b171d585ac7581f96938.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept becomes\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)\n$$\n\nand the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/ec/ec7207a7bc6a224d32c5ed5f6c77eb30f093beb4cfda03eea1d8da0926befe8a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "055bdf27-d354-57d7-96a6-de3300053035" :question/math-academy-id "e-1225" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^x$. What is the value of $k$?\n\n![](images/24/247e8b4712be05f38cfba373d930c5c8ee65ab64c9619e4ee74ec488fbd0c61d.png)" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=k\\left(\\dfrac25\\right)^x\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)^x\n$$\n\nand stretch it vertically by a scale factor of $k$. Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$.\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k)$, we see that we must have $k=1.5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/id #uuid "edf79f97-9325-55a0-a78b-133bba524177" :question/math-academy-id "e-1803" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x$." :question/worked-solution "The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nhas a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$, grows rapidly for $x<0$, and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0$.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, another point on the graph is\n\n$$\n\\left(-1, 4\\right)\n$$\n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc34e3d-7f5a-5410-9872-37c1fdc40860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282086} {:answer/id #uuid "4f619c4c-928e-5b2c-a2f6-9650ca210e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282370} {:answer/id #uuid "726c1b73-a586-5d60-b744-3f50a3ac17d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282669} {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd21f5f-8b88-5095-9dca-4fe1f18d7abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0bec54-298c-5799-a502-9235e4f8885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f5ee281-743c-52e5-8ba6-b17dfb13fd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-167338" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89db535a-6b79-5ebd-9145-63d87ca7cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0c/0c5bf6531bf352da158771f92eaab5a3974ccd623d1bf29dbe3ecba287f61d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108576} {:answer/id #uuid "d6059712-91b1-50c4-aa84-900f962d173e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/19ca51c3db532b2be7bcc54ccfa3f68a79a287f546e36ad567be9fe04570f279.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108577} {:answer/id #uuid "505db4d7-f0d0-58f3-a8e7-7c6a30fc9bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/704f65b21f68be3d17c68f2775276f1b8d0eda9cc0b6bef8f6eb81e421fbb59c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108578} {:answer/id #uuid "62a4eefa-1a40-590b-a617-fb321d990ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/46bc524e5e2efa6a920e96d884787d28d6f2aa9b6cf822b9ad0500ae52c01cf9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108579} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6b99c9-e223-50ef-83d2-75e00c911e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0aa2acf1075cf580fa959fb69208fdeb18fa0883647735a6facb95c4f9f83f0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108580}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac995ca-f58e-5bde-adde-b315d41984d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type 